 ## Overview
-The Triangle-AABB Intersection (Separating Axis Theorem) is a geometric primitive test that determines whether a triangle overlaps with an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) by checking for separation along 15 candidate axes [S2, S7, unofficial][S10]. It is primarily used for frustum culling, spatial partitioning, and conservative surface voxelization to ensure gap-free coverage by marking any voxel partially or merely touched by a triangle [S6, S9, unofficial][S11]. Unlike the sibling [Triangle / Box Overlap Test (Conservative 26/6-separating)](3528-triangle-box-overlap-test-conservative-26-6-separating.md), which reduces the problem to plane overlap and 2D projection checks requiring fewer instructions, this SAT method tests 15 axes (3 box face normals, 1 triangle normal, and 9 cross products of triangle edges and box axes) to guarantee exact overlap detection without false positives [S1, unofficial][S11].
+The Triangle-AABB Intersection (Separating Axis Theorem) is a geometric primitive test that determines whether a triangle overlaps with an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) by checking for separation along 13 candidate axes [S2, unofficial][S7, unofficial][S10]. It is primarily used for frustum culling, spatial partitioning, and conservative surface voxelization to ensure gap-free coverage by marking any voxel partially or merely touched by a triangle [S6, unofficial][S9, unofficial][S11]. Unlike the sibling [Triangle / Box Overlap Test (Conservative 26/6-separating)](3528-triangle-box-overlap-test-conservative-26-6-separating.md), which reduces the problem to plane overlap and 2D projection checks requiring fewer instructions, this SAT method tests 13 axes (3 box face normals, 1 triangle normal, and 9 cross products of triangle edges and box axes) to guarantee exact overlap detection without false positives [S11].
 
